INT. SUBURBAN KITCHEN - LATE AFTERNOON

Linoleum, a wall calendar, a fridge plastered in drawings. A clock ticks over the stove.

HEIDI KOWALCZYK-MBEKI (40s, apron over work clothes) stirs a pot. Her son PETER "PETE" KOWALCZYK-MBEKI (10, grass-stained knees) sits at the table, picking at a scab.

HEIDI
Dinner in ten.

PETE
Mom, I'm hungry.

HEIDI
You're always hungry.

PETE
Because I'm growing.

HEIDI
^Don't use that on me.

A heavy knock at the front door. Both freeze. Heidi looks at the clock. Pete slides off his chair.

PETE (CONT'D)
I'll get it.

HEIDI
Pete, no.

He is already gone. Heidi wipes her hands. The knocking comes again, patient. Pete's voice drifts from the hall.

PETE (O.S.)
Hi. Who are you?

STRANGER (O.S.)
Hello, young man. Is your mother home?

Heidi's face tightens. She puts the wooden spoon on the counter and follows.

IN THE HALL

A tall man in a rumpled gray suit, HAMISH OYELOWO-DRUMMOND (50s, soft hat, a manila folder), stands on the doormat, smiling carefully. Pete peers up at him, curious.

HAMISH
My name is Hamish. I'm from the county adoption office.

HEIDI
We're not adopting.

HAMISH
No, ma'am. But you were adopted, weren't you? In 1988.

Heidi goes pale. Pete takes her hand.

HEIDI
Who sent you?

HAMISH
Your sister.

PETE
Mom, I didn't know you had a sister.

HEIDI
Neither did I.

She steps aside. Lets him in.
